1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,912
Aarav opens a broken TV remote.

2
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:03,847
No tubes.

3
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:05,488
No tangle of wires.

4
00:00:05,488 --> 00:00:08,839
Just a flat board and
one small black chip.

5
00:00:09,189 --> 00:00:11,959
How can one tiny chip
run the whole remote?

6
00:00:12,459 --> 00:00:17,213
Before chips, every transistor, resistor
and capacitor was a separate part,

7
00:00:17,213 --> 00:00:19,001
soldered together by hand.

8
00:00:19,351 --> 00:00:22,016
Each joint was a place
where things could fail.

9
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:25,833
In nineteen fifty eight,
Jack Kilby had a better idea.

10
00:00:25,833 --> 00:00:30,289
Make all the parts, and the wires
between them, together on one piece of

11
00:00:30,289 --> 00:00:31,249
semiconductor.

12
00:00:31,899 --> 00:00:34,673
This is an integrated circuit, or IC.

13
00:00:34,673 --> 00:00:36,909
Integrated means joined into one.

14
00:00:37,259 --> 00:00:41,647
Transistors, resistors, capacitors
and their connections are all built

15
00:00:41,647 --> 00:00:43,702
on one tiny chip of silicon.

16
00:00:43,702 --> 00:00:47,927
Since it is one single piece,
it is called a monolithic IC.

17
00:00:48,277 --> 00:00:52,280
In nineteen fifty nine, Robert Noyce
showed how to make such chips from

18
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:54,997
silicon in a way that
could be mass produced.

19
00:00:55,647 --> 00:00:59,693
As methods improved, more and
more parts fitted on one chip.

20
00:00:59,693 --> 00:01:04,596
Small scale, medium scale, large scale,
and very large scale integration.

21
00:01:04,946 --> 00:01:10,278
I Cs are small, cheap to make in bulk,
use little power, and are very reliable,

22
00:01:10,278 --> 00:01:13,134
because there are almost
no soldered joints.

23
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:17,799
But they cannot handle large power,
and big coils are hard to build inside

24
00:01:17,799 --> 00:01:18,699
them.

25
00:01:19,006 --> 00:01:22,021
Now put a whole computer
brain on one chip.

26
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:23,805
That is a microprocessor.

27
00:01:24,155 --> 00:01:25,758
It has three main parts.

28
00:01:26,108 --> 00:01:31,442
The arithmetic and logic unit, or ALU,
does the maths and makes comparisons.

29
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:33,698
The control unit is the boss.

30
00:01:33,698 --> 00:01:37,881
It fetches each instruction, decodes
it, and tells the other parts what

31
00:01:37,881 --> 00:01:38,781
to do.

32
00:01:38,850 --> 00:01:43,525
And registers are tiny, super fast
stores that hold the numbers being worked

33
00:01:43,525 --> 00:01:44,592
on right now.

34
00:01:45,242 --> 00:01:48,004
But a microprocessor cannot work alone.

35
00:01:48,004 --> 00:01:53,171
Memory and input output chips sit outside
it, joined by sets of wires called

36
00:01:53,171 --> 00:01:54,071
buses.

37
00:01:54,479 --> 00:01:58,513
The first commercial microprocessor
came in nineteen seventy one.

38
00:01:58,513 --> 00:02:02,946
It handled four bits at a time and
had about two thousand three hundred

39
00:02:02,946 --> 00:02:03,854
transistors.

40
00:02:04,504 --> 00:02:07,167
A microcontroller goes one step further.

41
00:02:07,517 --> 00:02:12,084
It packs the processor, the memory,
input output ports and timers, all

42
00:02:12,084 --> 00:02:13,214
onto one chip.

43
00:02:13,564 --> 00:02:17,493
It is a complete small computer,
built to do one job well.

44
00:02:17,493 --> 00:02:19,780
Like the one inside Aarav's remote.

45
00:02:20,430 --> 00:02:23,856
Let's give a microcontroller
a job on the breadboard.

46
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:25,311
Blink a small lamp.

47
00:02:25,661 --> 00:02:27,802
The program has just four lines.

48
00:02:27,802 --> 00:02:28,702
Pin on.

49
00:02:28,499 --> 00:02:29,399
Wait.

50
00:02:29,042 --> 00:02:29,942
Pin off.

51
00:02:29,788 --> 00:02:30,688
Wait.

52
00:02:30,330 --> 00:02:31,270
Then repeat.

53
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:34,116
Watch the program step from line to line.

54
00:02:34,116 --> 00:02:38,371
When the pin turns on, it gives five
volts, and current flows through the

55
00:02:38,371 --> 00:02:39,271
lamp.

56
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:43,727
When the pin turns off, it drops to
zero volts, and the lamp goes dark.

57
00:02:44,077 --> 00:02:46,043
The scope shows a square wave.

58
00:02:46,043 --> 00:02:48,625
The program and the voltage move together.

59
00:02:49,275 --> 00:02:53,285
Change one number in the wait
line, and the lamp blinks faster.

60
00:02:53,285 --> 00:02:55,034
Same chip, new behaviour.

61
00:02:55,034 --> 00:02:56,108
Just new code.

62
00:02:56,758 --> 00:02:59,499
A microprocessor is a computer's brain.

63
00:02:59,499 --> 00:03:03,485
A microcontroller is a whole
small computer on one chip.

64
00:03:04,285 --> 00:03:06,945
So how much memory can a processor reach?

65
00:03:06,945 --> 00:03:09,060
It depends on its address lines.

66
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:13,020
Each address line is a wire
that can be zero or one.

67
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,042
One line gives two addresses.

68
00:03:15,742 --> 00:03:17,185
Two lines give four.

69
00:03:17,185 --> 00:03:18,774
Three lines give eight.

70
00:03:18,774 --> 00:03:20,964
Every new line doubles the count.

71
00:03:21,664 --> 00:03:25,267
So n address lines give two
to the power n addresses.

72
00:03:25,967 --> 00:03:27,143
Worked example.

73
00:03:27,143 --> 00:03:29,778
A processor has sixteen address lines.

74
00:03:29,778 --> 00:03:31,826
Each address holds one byte.

75
00:03:32,176 --> 00:03:36,653
Two to the power sixteen is sixty
five thousand, five hundred and thirty

76
00:03:36,653 --> 00:03:37,553
six.

77
00:03:37,857 --> 00:03:42,220
Divide by one thousand and twenty
four bytes per kilobyte, and you get

78
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:43,678
sixty four kilobytes.

79
00:03:44,328 --> 00:03:45,972
So which one goes where?

80
00:03:45,972 --> 00:03:47,710
Compare them side by side.

81
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:51,885
A microprocessor keeps memory
and input output outside.

82
00:03:51,885 --> 00:03:54,358
A microcontroller has them inside.

83
00:03:54,708 --> 00:03:58,592
A microprocessor is built
for many jobs, like a laptop.

84
00:03:58,592 --> 00:04:02,959
A microcontroller is built for
one job, like a washing machine.

85
00:04:03,309 --> 00:04:08,397
So a microcontroller is smaller, cheaper
and uses less power for its task.

86
00:04:09,047 --> 00:04:10,513
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:10,863 --> 00:04:15,067
Many students write that a
microprocessor has memory built in.

88
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:16,163
It does not.

89
00:04:16,513 --> 00:04:20,962
Memory, ports and timers on the
same chip is a microcontroller.

90
00:04:20,962 --> 00:04:23,475
A microprocessor needs them outside.

91
00:04:24,125 --> 00:04:25,811
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:25,811 --> 00:04:27,496
In PUC board papers.

93
00:04:27,496 --> 00:04:32,483
Name the parts of a microprocessor,
compare it with a microcontroller,

94
00:04:32,483 --> 00:04:34,994
or list the advantages of I Cs.

95
00:04:35,644 --> 00:04:36,544
Try this.

96
00:04:36,455 --> 00:04:40,721
A microcontroller has twelve address
lines, and each address holds one

97
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:41,621
byte.

98
00:04:41,275 --> 00:04:43,363
How many bytes can it address?

99
00:04:48,763 --> 00:04:49,663
C.

100
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:51,868
Four thousand and ninety six bytes.

101
00:04:52,218 --> 00:04:55,532
n address lines give two
to the power n addresses.

102
00:04:55,882 --> 00:04:59,286
Two to the power twelve is
four thousand and ninety six.

103
00:04:59,636 --> 00:05:04,059
With one byte each, that is four
thousand and ninety six bytes, or four

104
00:05:04,059 --> 00:05:04,959
kilobytes.

105
00:05:05,481 --> 00:05:07,002
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:12,705
Next, applications of electronics.

107
00:05:12,705 --> 00:05:18,073
How does one idea, controlling electrons,
run phones, radar and hospitals?
